1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,883
lage: a small programming language designed for L L M agents to write, run, and debug.

2
00:00:05,883 --> 00:00:10,536
This is a short tour of what makes it agent shaped, and what we measured.

3
00:00:11,540 --> 00:00:13,540
Agents make particular mistakes.

4
00:00:13,540 --> 00:00:16,602
They slip on indentation, precedence and quoting.

5
00:00:16,602 --> 00:00:19,601
Side effects hide inside innocent looking calls.

6
00:00:19,601 --> 00:00:24,976
And most languages report one error per run, as free text, which makes fix loops slow.

7
00:00:25,980 --> 00:00:26,767
Here is lage.

8
00:00:26,767 --> 00:00:30,763
The grammar is just brackets, with no operators and no precedence.

9
00:00:30,763 --> 00:00:36,273
A function states its contract: a precondition with requires, a postcondition with ensures.

10
00:00:36,273 --> 00:00:38,695
And the test sits right beside the code.

11
00:00:38,695 --> 00:00:43,357
If the output is truncated, an unclosed bracket is detected as a parse error.

12
00:00:44,360 --> 00:00:49,443
The checker reports every problem in a single pass, before anything runs.

13
00:00:49,443 --> 00:00:56,058
On a deliberately broken example it finds all 3 errors at once, with line and column, and a did

14
00:00:56,058 --> 00:00:57,033
you mean hint.

15
00:00:57,033 --> 00:01:00,793
Every failure is a JSON diagnostic, not a stack trace.

16
00:01:01,790 --> 00:01:03,182
The pipeline is simple.

17
00:01:03,182 --> 00:01:08,631
Source is parsed, then the whole program is checked, including imports and optional types.

18
00:01:08,631 --> 00:01:14,322
The default backend transpiles to Python and runs it, while a closure interpreter stays as the

19
00:01:14,322 --> 00:01:14,928
reference.

20
00:01:14,928 --> 00:01:18,802
Both are differential tested, and everything comes back as JSON.

21
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:23,742
Side effects must be declared by the function and granted by the run.

22
00:01:23,742 --> 00:01:28,883
Loops are bounded by a fuel budget and a call depth cap, so a runaway program ends with an

23
00:01:28,883 --> 00:01:30,540
error code instead of a hang.

24
00:01:30,540 --> 00:01:35,168
There are no exceptions, and nothing is non deterministic unless it is an effect.

25
00:01:36,170 --> 00:01:36,630
Speed.

26
00:01:36,630 --> 00:01:43,072
The Python transpiling backend is between 3.4 and 95 times faster than the reference

27
00:01:43,072 --> 00:01:43,992
interpreter.

28
00:01:43,992 --> 00:01:51,124
Computing fib of twenty seven takes 6.0 seconds in the interpreter and 0.06 in the transpiled

29
00:01:51,124 --> 00:01:51,738
backend.

30
00:01:52,740 --> 00:01:59,691
The repo has 323 unit tests, run on both backends, and a bundled standard library of 6 pure

31
00:01:59,691 --> 00:02:02,518
lage modules with 71 typed functions.

32
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:04,416
Honest caveats.

33
00:02:04,416 --> 00:02:08,835
This is a Python reference implementation, with no native compilation yet.

34
00:02:08,835 --> 00:02:14,269
Functions nested more than about ninety deep inside functions need the interpreter backend.

35
00:02:14,269 --> 00:02:19,346
The benchmark ran on a shared, loaded laptop, so compare the ratios, not the seconds.

36
00:02:20,350 --> 00:02:20,762
lage.

37
00:02:20,762 --> 00:02:25,289
The code, the tests, and the benchmark are in the repo.

